{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-162898",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+2y-6z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2x-3y+2z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2,-6⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2,-3,2⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 2=-3\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ -6=2\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{7}{2\\mu } \\\\ y=-\\frac{7}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{49}{4{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{49}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{49}{2{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+2z & =0 \\\\ 2(1)-3(-1)+2z & =0 \\\\ 5+2z & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+2z & =0 \\\\ 2(-1)-3(1)+2z & =0 \\\\ -5+2z & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,-1,-\\frac{5}{2}),\\,(-1,1,\\frac{5}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,-1,-\\frac{5}{2})$ | $(-1,1,\\frac{5}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $14$ | $-14$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,-1,-\\frac{5}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $14.$",
